Create Group 06 — Overlays & Surfaces for the DevKit.

This group belongs to:

Layer 01 — Foundations & Interface Primitives.

Existing groups:

Group 01 — Interaction Controls
Group 02 — Form Controls
Group 03 — Selection Controls
Group 04 — Navigation Controls
Group 05 — Feedback & Status

Use the DevKit Core Design Constitution as the immutable global foundation.

Do not redefine global tokens.

Inherit all existing:
- colors
- semantic colors
- typography
- spacing
- sizing
- control heights
- radius
- borders
- elevation
- shadows
- iconography
- motion
- responsive breakpoints
- accessibility
- focus treatment
- touch targets
- density
- Light theme
- Dark theme
- High Contrast
- RTL
- localization
- reduced motion
- reduced transparency

Create exactly these 10 components:

051 Tooltip
052 Popover
053 Dropdown Menu
054 Context Menu
055 Menu
056 Dialog
057 Alert Dialog
058 Drawer
059 Bottom Sheet
060 Modal Surface

These must be production-grade overlay primitives.

Do not create:
- application-specific dialogs
- checkout modals
- authentication dialogs
- complete settings panels
- complete sidebars
- application shells
- custom notification centers
- page-level overlays

Those must be composed from these primitives.

The overlay system must establish a consistent hierarchy.

Use the following conceptual hierarchy:

Tooltip:
brief supplementary explanation.

Popover:
contextual content attached to an element.

Dropdown Menu:
action/command collection opened from a trigger.

Context Menu:
context-specific actions invoked from contextual interaction.

Menu:
general structured collection of commands or choices.

Dialog:
focused interaction requiring temporary attention.

Alert Dialog:
high-importance confirmation, warning, or destructive decision.

Drawer:
side-oriented temporary surface.

Bottom Sheet:
mobile-friendly bottom-oriented temporary surface.

Modal Surface:
generic elevated temporary surface for focused content.

Do not treat all overlays as interchangeable.

Every overlay must have:

- predictable opening behavior
- predictable closing behavior
- clear focus behavior
- correct keyboard behavior
- correct screen-reader semantics
- viewport boundary handling
- responsive positioning
- appropriate dismissal behavior

Overlays must never open partially outside the viewport.

When there is insufficient space:
reposition
resize
scroll
or transform

rather than allowing important content to disappear.

